naru {{.Tag}}, built from commit {{.Commit}} by the release workflow on a GitHub-hosted ubuntu-24.04 runner, inside the builder container of `image/builder`.

Assets: `naru-host-{{.Tag}}.qcow2` (the boot disk of `naru-host`), `naru-host-upgrade-{{.Tag}}.tar` (the upgrade bundle: the kernel with its initramfs inside, and a manifest that names its version and SHA-256, which an A/B upgrade writes to the boot disk's trial slot), `naru-data-{{.Tag}}.qcow2` (the data disk both Nodes attach), `naru-node-kernel-{{.Tag}}`, `naru-node-initramfs-{{.Tag}}` and `naru-node-{{.Tag}}.json` (the Node boot set: the kernel and initramfs of the guest form that runs a Node as a microVM, and the manifest that names them with their SHA-256), `naru-orch-ext-{{.Tag}}.sqfs` and `naru-orch-ext-{{.Tag}}.json` (the `naru-orch` system extension that carries `naru-orchd` and `naru-signer`, and its description: the `sha256` field is what `spec.extensions` names, and the image is built for this release's version only), `naru-volumes-ext-{{.Tag}}.sqfs` and `naru-volumes-ext-{{.Tag}}.json` (the `naru-volumes` system extension that carries the volume add-on's manifest and image, and its description: a Node's configuration lists it with no digest, and the image is built for this release's version only), `naru-guest-{{.Tag}}.json` (the names and SHA-256 of the guest kernel and initrds the boot disk carries), `naru-inputs-{{.Tag}}.json` (the Firecracker digest and the test image reference that `naru-cluster` reads), `naructl-{{.Tag}}-linux-amd64` (the operator's tool: it generates a cluster CA, each Node's user-data and client certificates, and it is the Node API's client; a static binary), `naru-cluster-{{.Tag}}-linux-amd64` (the command that boots Nodes under QEMU for an external harness, a static binary: download it, check it against `SHA256SUMS` and make it executable), `naru-node-1.test.yaml` and `naru-node-2.test.yaml` (test-only user-data for Proxmox that runs gaon), `naru-sbom-{{.Tag}}.spdx.json` (the software bill of materials, SPDX 2.3 JSON: every package pinned under `image/` with its version, download location, SHA-256 and licence, and the Go modules each shipped Go binary was built from), `naru-sources-{{.Tag}}.tar` (the corresponding source: the exact upstream tarball of every GPL-, LGPL- and AGPL-licensed package of the image, and a `git archive` of commit {{.Commit}}), `SHA256SUMS` of the other {{.ListedCount}}, and `SHA256SUMS.sigstore.json` (the cosign signature of `SHA256SUMS`, which `SHA256SUMS` does not list because it signs it).

- **What these notes describe.** The assets of this release, how to use them and how to verify them. What changed since an earlier release is the commit history between the two tags. init serves the Node API (version, services, service logs, the stored configuration, and applying a change live or staged for the next boot) over mutual TLS on `spec.api.listen`, `0.0.0.0:50000` by default, and `naructl` is its client: `version`, `services`, `logs`, `get config` and `apply`. `naru-init` reports the release's tag as its version. A Node whose user-data has no `spec.api` serves no API and logs that the Node API is off.
- **The Node boot set and the image's room.** `naructl host boot-set add naru-node-{{.Tag}}.json --config <host client config>` adds the boot set to a host that runs mvmd, with no reboot, under the name `naru-node-` and the tag in lower case with each character outside a-z, 0-9 and `-` replaced by `-`; the files it names are read from beside the manifest. The boot disk's ESP is {{.ESPBytes}} bytes, and the largest kernel a Node installed from this image can take as an in-place upgrade is {{.UpgradeKernelBytes}} bytes.
- **The data disk carries a test-only TLS authority, private key included.** `inputs/registry/key.pem` is the private key of the CA that `inputs/registry/ca.pem` and `cert.pem` belong to. It is generated for each build, is trusted only by the mvmd on the Node that holds the disk, and the registry that uses it listens on 127.0.0.1. Anyone can read the key from this public asset; do not trust the CA anywhere else.
- **The user-data assets are test-only and carry private keys.** `naru-node-1.test.yaml` and `naru-node-2.test.yaml` hold the bootstrap tokens of both Nodes in `spec.files`, and `naru-node-1.test.yaml` also holds, in `spec.pki`, a test-only TLS authority, private key included: the CA derived from the release's version (ADR 0078), from which init issues node-1's pairs at boot and gaon's controllers sign the Node Agents' certificate requests. The CA is valid ten years from the epoch of the build and the certificates one year. Anyone can read that key and those tokens from this public asset: do not trust the CA anywhere else, and use the files on a throwaway deployment only. Two builds of one tree write the same files, and the CA and the tokens derive from the release's version, so anyone can compute them. They leave the Node API off (no `spec.api`). To enable it, make the API's authority in the CA's directory and write the user-data from that directory: `naructl gen api-ca --ca-dir <ca-dir>` (the cluster's CA directory, which `gen ca` wrote), then `naructl gen node --ca-dir <ca-dir> ...`, which adds `spec.api` when `<ca-dir>/api/` exists, then `naructl gen api-client --ca-dir <ca-dir> --role admin --endpoint <node-ip> --out client.yaml`, and `naructl version --config client.yaml`. A `reader` role reads and cannot apply.
- **The by-design assets are not byte-reproducible ({{.ByDesign}}).** The data disk carries a registry TLS authority, certificate and key generated anew for every build, and they are the only files that differ between two builds of one tree. Pin the digest of the asset published here, not the digest of a rebuild.
- **The root bundle.** The image's `/etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt` is curl's cacert.pem of {{.CACertDate}} (pinned by digest in `image/pkgs/cacert/pin.env`); a registry whose chain that bundle does not verify needs its authority in the Node's configuration (`gen node --registry host=H,ca=FILE`).
- **Relation to a workstation build.** `make release` runs in the builder container, whose base image, packages, Go and Rust installer are pinned by digest or version, so the runner's own toolchain reaches no asset. Build the commit with `make release VERSION={{.Tag}}` on a workstation with Docker and compare `SHA256SUMS`: every line is equal except those of the by-design assets ({{.ByDesign}}), which differ in each build. `make image-repro REFERENCE=<this SHA256SUMS> VERSION={{.Tag}}` does the comparison.
- **Verify the release.** `SHA256SUMS` is signed keyless by this workflow's identity, and every asset it lists has a build provenance attestation. Download `SHA256SUMS` and `SHA256SUMS.sigstore.json` and the assets you use, then run:

  ```
  cosign verify-blob --bundle SHA256SUMS.sigstore.json --certificate-identity 'https://github.com/garamsh/naru/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/{{.Tag}}' --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com SHA256SUMS
  sha256sum --check --ignore-missing SHA256SUMS
  gh attestation verify <asset> -R garamsh/naru
  ```

  A release that a manual run (`workflow_dispatch`) published carries the ref that run started from in the identity instead of `refs/tags/{{.Tag}}`. `docs/operate/verify-a-release.md` has the details.
- **A tag publishes once.** A second run for this tag, on the same commit, compares its `SHA256SUMS` with this release's and uploads nothing when they agree on every asset but the by-design ones ({{.ByDesign}}), which differ in each build; it fails and names the asset when any other differs.
{{if .Recreate}}- **A Node installed from an earlier image cannot take this release's upgrade bundle, and must be recreated.** This release's kernel is larger than the trial slot of the ESP of the previous release's image. Recreate the Node from `naru-host-{{.Tag}}.qcow2`. A Node installed from this image has room for a trial copy of a kernel twice the size of this release's own.
{{end}}
